        "lf_sha256": "7925618a142c1514a962d18e4c580f39fa4bc85426ba98de4c84a8b90e6bdc42"
      },
      "qualified_LF_slice": {
        "path": "E:/Samplinglib/runs/20261007-companion-priority/pbps-real-defect-root61/next-macro-source62/primary-A2-E12-dffe0a792ef0.LF.snapshot",
        "raw_bytes": 1794,
        "lf_bytes": 1794,
        "raw_sha256": "7925618a142c1514a962d18e4c580f39fa4bc85426ba98de4c84a8b90e6bdc42",
        "lf_sha256": "7925618a142c1514a962d18e4c580f39fa4bc85426ba98de4c84a8b90e6bdc42"
      },
      "byte_range": [
        775053,
        776847
      ],
      "literal_source_text": "\\rho_{\\rm mac}\\coloneqq\\frac{1-\\alpha\\eta}{1+\\alpha\\eta}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\gamma_{\\rm gap}\\coloneqq\\sqrt{1-\\rho_{\\rm mac}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{\\alpha\\eta}}{1+\\alpha\\eta}\\,. (B.12)"
    },
    {
      "id": "S1.p1",
      "coverage": "NODE",
      "reason": "Original global C2 potential/two Hessian bounds and positive alpha order; actual consumer binder context.",
      "qualified_raw_slice": {
        "path": "E:/Samplinglib/runs/20261007-companion-priority/pbps-real-defect-root61/next-macro-source62/primary-S1-p1-dffe0a792ef0.raw.snapshot",
        "raw_bytes": 3603,
        "lf_bytes": 3603,
        "raw_sha256": "a9bc657fb926368561ab2657dfbfbf6a7a0ba5b8c2122f8d1fb8510a0a975262",
        "lf_sha256": "a9bc657fb926368561ab2657dfbfbf6a7a0ba5b8c2122f8d1fb8510a0a975262"
      },
      "qualified_LF_slice": {
        "path": "E:/Samplinglib/runs/20261007-companion-priority/pbps-real-defect-root61/next-macro-source62/primary-S1-p1-dffe0a792ef0.LF.snapshot",
        "raw_bytes": 3603,
        "lf_bytes": 3603,
        "raw_sha256": "a9bc657fb926368561ab2657dfbfbf6a7a0ba5b8c2122f8d1fb8510a0a975262",
        "lf_sha256": "a9bc657fb926368561ab2657dfbfbf6a7a0ba5b8c2122f8d1fb8510a0a975262"
      },
      "byte_range": [
        47126,
        50729
      ],
      "literal_source_text": "We study the problem of sampling from the probability measure \\mu(\\mathrm{d}x)=Z^{-1}e^{-V(x)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x on \\mathbb{R}^{d} , where V\\in C^{2}(\\mathbb{R}^{d}) satisfies, for 0<\\alpha\\leq\\beta , \\alpha I\\preceq\\nabla^{2}V(x)\\preceq\\beta I\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptx\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\kappa\\coloneqq\\tfrac{\\beta}{\\alpha}\\,. (1.1) The main result of this paper is the following."
    },
    {
      "id": "S2.E6",
      "coverage": "NODE",
      "reason": "Normalized independent Gaussian joint law actual J/nu context.",
      "qualified_raw_slice": {
        "path": "E:/Samplinglib/runs/20261007-companion-priority/pbps-real-defect-root61/next-macro-source62/primary-S2-E6-dffe0a792ef0.raw.snapshot",
        "raw_bytes": 1993,
        "lf_bytes": 1993,
        "raw_sha256": "1f01e27c8eb38fd05a57d820c65ee6b36832171cce845e8cd1b52f4588526375",
        "lf_sha256": "1f01e27c8eb38fd05a57d820c65ee6b36832171cce845e8cd1b52f4588526375"
      },
      "qualified_LF_slice": {
        "path": "E:/Samplinglib/runs/20261007-companion-priority/pbps-real-defect-root61/next-macro-source62/primary-S2-E6-dffe0a792ef0.LF.snapshot",
        "raw_bytes": 1993,
        "lf_bytes": 1993,
        "raw_sha256": "1f01e27c8eb38fd05a57d820c65ee6b36832171cce845e8cd1b52f4588526375",
        "lf_sha256": "1f01e27c8eb38fd05a57d820c65ee6b36832171cce845e8cd1b52f4588526375"
      },
      "byte_range": [
        96222,
        98215
      ],
      "literal_source_text": "\\pi_{\\eta}(\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y)\\propto\\exp\\Bigl\\{-V(x)-\\frac{\\|x-y\\|^{2}}{2\\eta}\\Bigr\\}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,. (2.6)"
    },
    {
      "id": "S2.E7",
      "coverage": "NODE",
      "reason": "Same normalized conditional law; actual S definition context.",
      "qualified_raw_slice": {
        "path": "E:/Samplinglib/runs/20261007-companion-priority/pbps-real-defect-root61/next-macro-source62/primary-S2-E7-dffe0a792ef0.raw.snapshot",
        "raw_bytes": 1473,
        "lf_bytes": 1473,
        "raw_sha256": "2be554d84baf1f2b796ade05fba49271b5d05e4e8d2d46427d47a6756f85c8f9",
        "lf_sha256": "2be554d84baf1f2b796ade05fba49271b5d05e4e8d2d46427d47a6756f85c8f9"
      },
      "qualified_LF_slice": {
        "path": "E:/Samplinglib/runs/20261007-companion-priority/pbps-real-defect-root61/next-macro-source62/primary-S2-E7-dffe0a792ef0.LF.snapshot",
        "raw_bytes": 1473,
        "lf_bytes": 1473,
        "raw_sha256": "2be554d84baf1f2b796ade05fba49271b5d05e4e8d2d46427d47a6756f85c8f9",
        "lf_sha256": "2be554d84baf1f2b796ade05fba49271b5d05e4e8d2d46427d47a6756f85c8f9"
      },
      "byte_range": [
        98826,
        100299
      ],
      "literal_source_text": "X\\sim\\mu\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptZ\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I)\\ \\text{independently}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptY=X+\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,Z\\,. (2.7)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.SS1.p1",
      "coverage": "NODE",
      "reason": "Real joint space/conditional projection context; extension to canonical scalar is ASTIS adapter, not source graph substitution.",
      "qualified_raw_slice": {
        "path": "E:/Samplinglib/runs/20261007-companion-priority/pbps-real-defect-root61/next-macro-source62/primary-A2-SS1-p1-dffe0a792ef0.raw.snapshot",
        "raw_bytes": 19341,
        "lf_bytes": 19341,
        "raw_sha256": "395059e86bc44a9581440d09e7095e1979f6579e14a55979f5bd2fa382b6fb24",
        "lf_sha256": "395059e86bc44a9581440d09e7095e1979f6579e14a55979f5bd2fa382b6fb24"
      },
      "qualified_LF_slice": {
        "path": "E:/Samplinglib/runs/20261007-companion-priority/pbps-real-defect-root61/next-macro-source62/primary-A2-SS1-p1-dffe0a792ef0.LF.snapshot",
        "raw_bytes": 19341,
        "lf_bytes": 19341,
        "raw_sha256": "395059e86bc44a9581440d09e7095e1979f6579e14a55979f5bd2fa382b6fb24",
        "lf_sha256": "395059e86bc44a9581440d09e7095e1979f6579e14a55979f5bd2fa382b6fb24"
      },
      "byte_range": [
        708364,
        727705
      ],
      "literal_source_text": "Our target distribution is the joint law \\pi_{\\eta} defined in ( 2.6 ), and we set \\mathfrak{H}\\coloneqq L^{2}(\\pi_{\\eta}) . Recall from ( 3.11 ) that the ideal Proximal BPS has transition kernel \\mathcal{K}\\bigl((x,y),\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}y^{\\prime}\\bigr)=\\delta_{2x-y}(\\mathrm{d}y^{\\prime})\\,\\bigl[\\rho\\,\\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y^{\\prime}}(\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime})+(1-\\rho)\\,\\mathcal{H}_{y^{\\prime}}(x,\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime})\\bigr]\\,, where \\mathcal{H}_{y^{\\prime}} is the conditional half-turn kernel from Proposition 3.2 . Since the algorithm is a discrete-time chain, its evolution is described by the induced one-step Markov operator on observables, (\\mathsf{K}f)(x,y)\\coloneqq\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^{2d}}f(x^{\\prime},y^{\\prime})\\,\\mathcal{K}\\bigl((x,y),\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}y^{\\prime}\\bigr)\\,. Thus \\mathcal{K} acts on probability laws, whereas \\mathsf{K} is its induced action on observables. The first term in brackets in \\mathcal{K} is the conditional resampling branch. After the auxiliary coordinate is updated to y^{\\prime} , this branch replaces the x -coordinate by an independent draw from \\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y^{\\prime}} . The associated conditional expectation operator is (\\mathsf{P}f)(x,y)\\coloneqq\\int f(x^{\\prime},y)\\,\\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y}(\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime})=\\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\\mid Y=y]\\,. (B.1) In particular, \\mathsf{P}f depends only on the coordinate y . The range and kernel of \\mathsf{P} are \\begin{split}\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}}&\\coloneqq\\operatorname{ran}(\\mathsf{P})=\\{f\\in\\mathfrak{H}:f(x,y)=g(y)\\text{ for some }g\\in L^{2}(\\pi_{\\eta}^{Y})\\}\\,,\\\\ \\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp}&\\coloneqq\\ker(\\mathsf{P})=\\operatorname{ran}(\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp})=\\{f\\in\\mathfrak{H}:\\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\\mid Y]=0\\}\\,.\\end{split} (B.2) Here \\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}\\coloneqq I-\\mathsf{P} , so \\mathfrak{H}=\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}}\\oplus\\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp} and f=\\mathsf{P}f+\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}f . In the common terminology of hypocoercivity [ 14 , 21 ] , \\operatorname{ran}(\\mathsf{P}) is the macroscopic subspace and its orthogonal complement is the microscopic subspace. Thus functions in \\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}} depend only on the coordinate y , whereas functions in \\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp} have zero mean under \\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y} for \\pi_{\\eta}^{Y} -almost every y ."
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.SS1.p2",
      "coverage": "EXCLUDED",
      "reason": "Block target/source conventions preserve future macro boundary.",
